[Extra Perfect Number]
    Binary representation starts and ends with 1 (no internal zeros? Actually extra perfect means
    binary representation has 1 at both ends only, i.e., the number is of the form 2ⁿ+1.)
    Example: 3 (11), 5 (101), 9 (1001), 17 (10001) → all are 2ⁿ+1.

    Steps:
        1. Check if n-1 is a power of two: (n-1) & (n-2) == 0  (and n>1).
        2. If yes → Extra perfect.

    Solution:
        #include <stdio.h>
        int main() {
            int n;
            scanf("%d", &n);
            if (n > 1 && ((n-1) & (n-2)) == 0)
                printf("Extra perfect number\n");
            else
                printf("Not Extra perfect number\n");
            return 0;
        }

