[Pentagon Number]
    Definition: A number that can be represented as dots forming a regular pentagon.
    Formula: Pn = n(3n-1)/2.
    To check if a number is pentagonal:
        Solve n = (sqrt(24x + 1) + 1) / 6. If n is an integer, it's pentagonal.

    Solution:
        #include <stdio.h>
        #include <math.h>
        int main() {
            int x;
            scanf("%d", &x);
            double n = (sqrt(24*x + 1) + 1) / 6;
            if (n == (int)n)
                printf("Pentagon number\n");
            else
                printf("Not Pentagon number\n");
            return 0;
        }

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

[Proth Number]
    A number of the form k * 2ⁿ + 1, where k is odd and k < 2ⁿ.
    To check: deduct 1, repeatedly divide by 2 while even, get k (odd).
    If k < 2ⁿ, then Proth.

    Solution:
        #include <stdio.h>
        int main() {
            int n, temp, odd;
            scanf("%d", &n);
            if (n <= 1) { printf("Not Proth\n"); return 0; }
            temp = n - 1;
            int pow2 = 1;
            while (temp % 2 == 0) {
                temp /= 2;
                pow2 *= 2;
            }
            odd = temp;   // k (must be odd)
            if (odd < pow2 && odd % 2 == 1)
                printf("Proth number\n");
            else
                printf("Not Proth number\n");
            return 0;
        }

